.TH XNVME-DRIVER 1 "19 October 2026" "xNVMe" "xNVMe"
.SH NAME
\fBxnvme-driver \fP- Bind the driver a backend needs, or show them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.fam C
\fBxnvme\fP \fIdriver\fP [\fB--be\fP <backend>] [<bdf>\.\.\. | \fB--all\fP] [\fB--force\fP] [\fB--dry-run\fP]
.fam T
.fi
.fam T
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Without \fB--be\fP, print '\fBxnvme\fP list' and '\fBxnvme\fP env': each function with its \fIdriver\fP, and the host setup the drivers depend on.
.PP
With \fB--be\fP, put each named function, or with \fB--all\fP every function not in use, on the \fIdriver\fP the backend needs: the kernel nvme \fIdriver\fP for the kernel backends, a userspace \fIdriver\fP for spdk, libvfn and upcie. A function whose namespaces are in use is refused; \fB--force\fP overrides only the refusal of a namespace carrying a filesystem or partition table. \fB--dry-run\fP changes nothing.
.RE
.PP


.SH SEE ALSO
Full documentation at: <https://xnvme.io/>
